---
/**
 * The bench's frame: the box the player sits in, the band along its bottom
 * edge, and the geometry the player's own parts are laid out with.
 *
 * The split with `BenchIsland.tsx` is deliberate and is the same split the
 * library itself draws. That file composes primitives, points them at a source
 * and reads what they report; this one decides what the result looks like.
 * Nothing about the composition is expressible here and nothing about the
 * appearance is expressible there, so neither file can quietly grow the
 * other's job.
 *
 * ---- why every selector below is `:global()` --------------------------------
 *
 * Astro scopes a component's rules by stamping an attribute onto the elements
 * in its own template, and the player's elements are not in it: they are
 * rendered by React inside the island, which no scope attribute reaches. So the
 * choice is not between scoped and global rules, it is between global rules and
 * rules that match nothing.
 *
 * `:global()` per selector rather than `<style is:global>` for the whole block,
 * so each rule states at its own head that it is addressing markup produced
 * elsewhere. The class names are this page's and appear nowhere else on the
 * site, which is what makes that safe.
 *
 * ---- why `client:only` and not a hydrating directive ------------------------
 *
 * `client:visible` and its siblings render the component on the server and then
 * hydrate it. Both halves of that are wrong here. The server-rendered half
 * would put a real `<button>` into the document that does nothing until a
 * script arrives, which is the "present and disabled" shape this whole site
 * argues against — and for a reader with JavaScript off it would never stop
 * being that. The hydrating half then has to reproduce, in a browser holding a
 * media element, markup that was produced where there was none to measure; any
 * disagreement between the two is a console error on the site's root document,
 * and console errors on that document are one of the things
 * `scripts/check-deploy-artifact.mjs` fails the deployed artifact on. That is a
 * risk this page has no reason to take.
 *
 * `client:only` renders nothing on the server, so neither problem exists, and a
 * reader whose script fails gets a settled page rather than a blank one — the
 * frame below and its fallback are static markup. What it costs is that the
 * readout under the frame is absent until the island mounts, and nothing
 * reserves its height. Stated rather than left to be discovered.
 *
 * ---- the two theme systems, and why they do not meet ------------------------
 *
 * `@playdeck/react/theme.css` is the library's own optional stylesheet, and
 * `DESIGN.md` puts it out of this system's scope. It is not imported here at
 * all: the skin switch loads and unloads it at a reader's request, and
 * `BenchIsland.tsx` carries how and why. What this file owes it is the mapping
 * below.
 *
 * They cannot bleed into each other, and that is a property of that file rather
 * than of this page's care. Every selector in it is inside `@layer playdeck`
 * and wrapped in `:where()`, so it matches only elements carrying a
 * `data-playdeck-part` attribute and loses to any unlayered rule here
 * regardless of specificity. In the other direction it declares no token of its
 * own: every value is read as `var(--playdeck-…, fallback)`, so the block below
 * is the whole of what this site says to it.
 *
 * The mapping is inert while the switch reads `none` — nothing reads a custom
 * property no stylesheet is asking for — which is what lets it be stated once
 * here rather than toggled. It spends this site's role tokens and writes no
 * colour, so rule 1 of `DESIGN.md` holds across the seam.
 *
 * ---- geometry is the page's, in both skin positions -------------------------
 *
 * The rules under `.bench__stage` are layout and nothing else, and they apply
 * whichever way the skin switch is set. That is not this page bending the rule
 * that `none` applies no CSS: it is the line the library itself draws. Its
 * stylesheet says "appearance only; layer geometry belongs to the components",
 * so that a player works with no stylesheet at all and so that a consumer can
 * put the controls somewhere other than over the picture. Where the layers sit
 * is therefore the consumer's decision in every composition the library ships
 * to, and on this page the consumer is this file.
 */
import Sweep from './Sweep.astro';
import BenchIsland from './BenchIsland';
import { benchSources } from '../bench-sources';

// Resolved against the configured base the way every other address on this
// site is, rather than written as a root-absolute literal.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

/*
 * The still the dormant player shows for the no-JavaScript fallback, and the
 * credit that goes with it — both read off `bench-sources.ts` rather than
 * written here, because that file bundles a provider's URL, poster, start
 * time and credit into one object precisely so a page reading any one of them
 * cannot pick up a different provider's value for another. This file used to
 * hold its own `posterUrl` constant and its own hardcoded credit paragraph,
 * back when both providers played the same film; with `youtube` and `vimeo`
 * now playing different films, a constant here would be exactly the "override
 * the source alone" mistake the module comment in `bench-sources.ts`
 * describes.
 *
 * The fallback link's target and the still it uses are both whichever
 * position is the switch's default. Resolved from `bench-sources.ts` rather
 * than written as a literal, so a change to the default position is one
 * file's edit rather than several.
 */
const fallback = benchSources.find((entry) => entry.ready);
if (fallback === undefined) {
  throw new Error('Bench.astro: no ready source for the no-JavaScript fallback.');
}
const fallbackUrl = fallback.source(base);
const poster = fallback.poster(base);
const credit = fallback.credit;
// `'width / height'`, not a rounded decimal: CSS computes the ratio those two
// integers describe exactly, so nothing here approximates a number a browser
// can compute itself. Written onto `#bench-stage` below as
// `--bench-aspect-ratio`, the same custom property `BenchIsland.tsx`'s
// `StagePortal` updates once a reader presses a different position -- so the
// box is the right shape before any script runs and stays the right shape
// after one does.
const aspectRatio = `${fallback.width} / ${fallback.height}`;
---

<div class="bench">
  {
    /* The instrument. `--elevation-instrument` and no border: `DESIGN.md`
     * rule 4 allows an elevated element exactly one of the two, and
     * border-under-blur is the pairing that stays banned. The allowlist names
     * this element, and only this element, as what may spend that token. */
  }
  <div class="bench__frame">
    {
      /* Where `StagePortal` in `BenchIsland.tsx` renders the player. It is
       * only ever the portal's target: it renders nothing of its own, and
       * with JavaScript blocked it holds the fallback below instead. */
    }
    <div
      class="bench__stage"
      id="bench-stage"
      style={`--bench-aspect-ratio: ${aspectRatio}`}
    >
      {
        /* With JavaScript the island is the whole player and this is inert
         * text the parser never fetches from. Without it, this is the whole
         * frame: the still, and a link to where the switch's default position
         * plays.
         *
         * There is no same-origin clip to hand a plain `<video>` any more, and
         * a hosted provider is an iframe embed rather than a file, so there is
         * nothing here for a browser with scripting off to attach to on its
         * own — that embed's own player is itself a script. A link is the
         * honest fallback: it costs one request, it costs it only on a click,
         * and it never claims to be running a player it cannot run.
         *
         * `<noscript>` is `display: none` while scripts run, so this is never
         * a second thing in the frame — it is the frame's only content exactly
         * when the island is not there. */
      }
      <noscript>
        <a class="bench__fallback" href={fallbackUrl}>
          {
            /* The same still the island's player shows, here as a real `<img>`
             * — not a CSS background, so this path keeps the guarantee the
             * rest of the page does. `srcset`/`sizes` for the same reason
             * `BenchIsland.tsx`'s `Player.PosterImage` carries them: this is
             * the largest image on the page, and a no-JavaScript reader on a
             * narrow or a high-density screen is owed the same match between
             * screen and file a scripted reader gets. */
          }
          <img
            class="bench__fallback-image"
            src={poster.src}
            srcset={poster.srcSet}
            sizes="100vw"
            alt=""
          />
          <span class="bench__fallback-label">Watch on {fallback.label} &rarr;</span>
        </a>
      </noscript>
    </div>
    {
      /* The one gradient, in its one permitted accent form, rendered once on
       * this page. `DESIGN.md` records that six sweeps down one page is the
       * exact creep the one-gradient rule exists to stop, so this is the only
       * call to this component here. */
    }
    <Sweep form="accent" />
  </div>

  {
    /* The credit CC BY asks for wherever this media plays, including the
     * no-JavaScript path above — which is why this is `<noscript>` rather
     * than a line the island prints unconditionally. It is not unwrapped
     * static markup: with a script running, `BenchIsland`'s own `Credit`
     * mounts in its place and this paragraph is `display: none`, because it
     * names only the switch's *default* film and a reader who has pressed the
     * source switch is looking at a different one. Naming the wrong film
     * would itself be the omission CC BY exists to prevent, only pointed the
     * other way — so this is the no-script case, not a fallback that also
     * happens to render first.
     *
     * Set at `--text-fn` in `--color-ink-subtle`, the same treatment
     * `.bench__quiet` gets: it qualifies the instrument rather than
     * captioning it, and it is not a footer, because CC BY asks for the
     * credit to be findable near the work rather than at the end of the
     * page. */
  }
  <noscript>
    <p class="bench__credit">
      <em>{credit.title}</em> &copy; {credit.holder}, licensed{' '}
      <a href={credit.licenceUrl} rel="license">
        {credit.licenceLabel}
      </a>.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  {
    /* The island renders the credit for whichever film is actually selected
     * (`Credit` in `BenchIsland.tsx`) as well as the line under the frame —
     * see `QuietLine` there. Neither is static markup here: the credit has to
     * say a different film's name the moment the source switch moves, and the
     * quiet line has to say something different once the clip has loaded, and
     * something different again once the source is somebody else's host. A
     * no-JavaScript reader gets neither — the credit above stands in for the
     * first, and the fallback link is a different player with its own
     * behaviour that the quiet line would be describing incorrectly. `e2e`
     * proves the resting claim: a spec records every request `/` makes at
     * rest and fails if one leaves this origin. */
  }
  <BenchIsland base={base} client:only="react" />
</div>

<style>
  .bench {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  /* The raised body of the instrument, and the one element on this site that
   * takes `--elevation-instrument`. Two rows: the picture, and the sweep along
   * the bottom edge. `overflow: hidden` is what makes the band the frame's own
   * edge rather than a strip lying under it. */
  .bench__frame {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: 1fr auto;
    overflow: hidden;
    background-color: var(--stage-field);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: var(--elevation-instrument);
  }

  /* The screen, and the reason a dormant instrument is worth looking at.
   *
   * Nothing is loaded until the affordance is pressed, so there is no frame to
   * show and this component may not invent one — no poster, no still, no
   * gradient standing in for a picture.
   *
   * The ratio is `--bench-aspect-ratio`, not a literal: the inline `style`
   * above sets it from `bench-sources.ts`'s own `width`/`height` for whichever
   * position the page rests on, and `StagePortal` in `BenchIsland.tsx` moves
   * it to match whenever a reader presses a different one. A literal 16:9 here
   * once left a 2.39:1 film letterboxed inside a box shaped for a different
   * ratio — the same class of defect as a poster from one film over a video
   * of another, measured in bars down the sides rather than in a wrong title.
   * The fallback, `2048 / 858`, is every current entry's own shape rather than
   * the library's generic 16:9 one, so there is no frame — scripted or not,
   * before or after a press — in which the box and the picture disagree. It
   * stops being right the day a film of a different shape joins this file,
   * which is the same day it would need to become source-derived rather than
   * written here; nothing today makes that necessary.
   *
   * Always dark. `--stage-*` never moves with `data-theme`, so the picture
   * reads the same in both themes — a cinema is dark regardless of the room. */
  .bench__stage {
    display: grid;
    aspect-ratio: var(--bench-aspect-ratio, 2048 / 858);
    background-color: var(--stage-sunken);

    /* ---- the player's tokens, spoken in this site's roles ----------------
     *
     * Set here rather than on the viewport because the theme reads each one
     * with `var(--name, default)` and never declares it, so a value on any
     * ancestor reaches every part. One element to read, and adding a control
     * later needs nothing added here. Inert until the skin switch loads the
     * stylesheet that reads them. */

    /* The well behind the media, which is what shows before a frame is
     * decoded. */
    --playdeck-color-backdrop: var(--stage-sunken);
    /* The control bar and the activation button share this pair: a raised
     * panel in the stage's own dark surface rather than the theme's
     * translucent black. `--stage-ink` on `--stage-surface` is the contrast
     * pair `DESIGN.md`'s dark table already records. */
    --playdeck-color-surface: var(--stage-surface);
    --playdeck-color-on-surface: var(--stage-ink);
    /* `--playdeck-overlay-scrim` is the control bar's background and its
     * default is a gradient. This system has exactly one gradient and
     * `Sweep.astro` draws it, so the bar takes the flat surface the rest of
     * the frame is built from. */
    --playdeck-overlay-scrim: var(--stage-surface);
    --playdeck-control-hover: var(--stage-sunken);
    --playdeck-color-accent: var(--stage-accent);
    /* One focus treatment for the whole site, and this is the belt to
     * `base.css`'s braces. The theme carries a rule that would draw a white
     * ring if it ever did the drawing, and its default is the only
     * `--playdeck-*` value that could put a colour on this page from outside
     * the system. */
    --playdeck-color-focus: var(--stage-accent);
    /* The seek slider's three grounds, and the one place here where the choice
     * is arithmetic rather than taste. On `--color-line`, the quieter
     * separator role, the loaded range measures below the 3:1 that non-text UI
     * owes; on the sunken well it clears it. The thumb takes a ring because
     * the accent does not clear 3:1 against both the track and the loaded
     * range. The ratios are in `DESIGN.md`. */
    --playdeck-color-track: var(--stage-sunken);
    --playdeck-color-buffered: var(--stage-line-strong);
    --playdeck-color-thumb-ring: var(--stage-ink);
    /* The time readout is machine output, so it is mono like every other value
     * on this site, at the caption rung — above the 11px floor. */
    --playdeck-font-family: var(--font-mono);
    --playdeck-font-size: var(--text-sm);
    --playdeck-font-size-small: var(--text-xs);
    --playdeck-space-1: var(--space-1);
    --playdeck-space-2: var(--space-2);
    --playdeck-space-3: var(--space-3);
    --playdeck-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    --playdeck-radius-large: var(--radius-md);
    --playdeck-control-size: var(--hit-target);
    --playdeck-transition-duration: var(--duration-fast);
    --playdeck-transition-easing: var(--ease);
  }

  /* One cell, and every layer in it.
   *
   * A single named area rather than absolute positioning, because that is what
   * makes the picture the thing that sizes the cell: the overlays are laid into
   * the same cell without taking it out of flow, so nothing here has to restate
   * a height the media already determines.
   *
   * The ratio is read from the property the library writes onto this element
   * once a provider has measured its media -- right to keep reading, because
   * the library knows the truth for a real `<video>` element where one exists.
   * What changed is the fallback. `16 / 9` was a guess about a film nobody had
   * measured; `var(--bench-aspect-ratio, 2048 / 858)` is the same property
   * `.bench__stage` reads above, so before a provider has measured anything --
   * and for `youtube`, which never does, see `MEDIA_ASPECT_RATIO_PROPERTY` in
   * `viewport-media.tsx` -- this cell is already the film's own shape rather
   * than the library's generic one. A stale fallback here is how a 2.39:1
   * poster ended up stretched to 16:9 inside this exact cell: the poster lives
   * in the `stack` area this element defines, so its box was this element's
   * box. */
  :global(.bench__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='viewport']) {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-areas: 'stack';
    aspect-ratio: var(--playdeck-media-aspect-ratio, var(--bench-aspect-ratio, 2048 / 858));
    inline-size: 100%;
  }

  :global(.bench__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='media']) {
    grid-area: stack;
    /* A grid item's automatic minimum is its content, and a video's content is
     * its intrinsic width. Without this the picture would refuse to shrink
     * below that and push the frame wider than the page. */
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  /* The activation affordance is an overlay, and this is what makes it behave
   * like one under the `none` skin.
   *
   * The library ships this part `position: absolute; inset: 0; margin: auto`
   * with no size, so with no stylesheet at all it is full-bleed — which is the
   * behaviour this page wants, because a click anywhere on the picture should
   * start the clip. What comes with it is the user agent's own button paint,
   * and measured on this frame that is an opaque `rgb(239, 239, 239)` fill and
   * a `2px outset` black border, at `z-index: 30`, over a poster at 10. The
   * still was loaded, decoded and marked `visible` by the library's poster
   * state machine, and painted over regardless.
   *
   * So this is not the page reaching into the skin. An element whose whole job
   * is to overlay a picture, and which occludes it instead, is not styled — it
   * is broken, and the browser rather than the library is what broke it. Both
   * declarations say only that: no size, no radius, no badge, no hover. Those
   * are chrome and they stay the theme's, which is what keeps the two skin
   * positions a contrast about chrome rather than about whether there is a
   * picture at all.
   *
   * ---- and this is why it is scoped rather than unconditional ---------------
   *
   * Written unscoped first, and that falsified the paragraph above. This rule
   * is unlayered while `theme.css` lives entirely inside `@layer playdeck`, and
   * an unlayered declaration beats a layered one for the same property whatever
   * the specificities are — the `[hidden]` reset below relies on exactly that.
   * So the reset reached the themed player too: the part computed
   * `rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)` under both skins, and `theme.css`'s own
   * `--playdeck-color-surface` fill never landed. Pressing `theme` gave a bare
   * glyph with no badge.
   *
   * `data-bench-skin` rides on the viewport, written by `Stage` in
   * `BenchIsland.tsx` in the same React commit as the state it reports, so the
   * rule matches only while the position it is written for is the one selected.
   * Under `theme` nothing here applies and the stylesheet does its own work.
   *
   * Written as the two `--playdeck-activation-*` tokens rather than as
   * `background-color`/`border`, because that is the only spelling that
   * reaches this part: the button states both properties inline as reads of
   * those tokens, and an inline declaration beats any stylesheet. */
  :global(.bench__stage)
    :global([data-bench-skin='none'] [data-playdeck-part='activation']) {
    --playdeck-activation-fill: transparent;
    --playdeck-activation-border: 0;
  }

  /* The other half of the same defect, on the other skin -- issue #552,
   * filed against the library rather than fixed there: under `theme`, this
   * part is sized into a roughly 4rem badge, so only the badge was pressable
   * and the rest of the picture did nothing. Measured directly: a click two
   * inches from the badge, well inside the frame, reached no control at all.
   *
   * The fix redraws the badge as a background rather than as the button's own
   * box, the technique `HeroPlayer.astro` used for the same part before this
   * page was rebuilt as a bench (`git show 61599a4855:apps/site/src/components/HeroPlayer.astro`):
   * the element itself goes full-bleed and transparent, and a `::before`
   * paints the badge in its place. `--space-8` is 4rem, so the face is the
   * size the theme drew it and the only thing that moved is the target. The
   * icon shares the badge's grid area and paints over it because it comes
   * later in the tree.
   *
   * `border-radius: 0` on the button's own box for the reason `HeroPlayer.astro`
   * records: the theme rounds this part to 50%, which on a 4rem square is the
   * badge's circle and on a full-bleed box is an ellipse inscribed in the
   * stage — and a rounded corner clips hit testing, not just paint. Squared
   * off here; the badge below carries its own roundness instead.
   *
   * Unlayered and scoped to `[data-bench-skin='theme']` for the same two
   * reasons the `none` block above is scoped rather than unconditional: this
   * beats `theme.css`'s own layered rule for the same property regardless of
   * specificity, and scoping it keeps `none` untouched -- that position is
   * already full-bleed with no badge at all, and drawing one there would be
   * this page adding chrome the `none` position exists to not have. */
  :global(.bench__stage)
    :global([data-bench-skin='theme'] [data-playdeck-part='activation']) {
    grid-area: stack;
    grid-template-areas: 'face';
    place-content: center;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: 100%;
    /* The token, not `background-color`: the button reads it inline, and an
     * inline declaration beats any stylesheet. Clearing it is what lets the
     * `::before` below be the only badge on the frame. */
    --playdeck-activation-fill: transparent;
    border-radius: 0;
    outline-offset: calc(-1 * var(--space-1));
  }

  :global(.bench__stage)
    :global(
      [data-bench-skin='theme'] [data-playdeck-part='activation']
    )::before {
    content: '';
    grid-area: face;
    inline-size: var(--space-8);
    block-size: var(--space-8);
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
  }

  :global(.bench__stage)
    :global(
      [data-bench-skin='theme'] [data-playdeck-part='activation'] > svg
    ) {
    grid-area: face;
  }

  /* The other direction: once the clip is running, the same gesture toggles
   * it. `SurfaceToggle` in `BenchIsland.tsx` is `Player.PlayButton` with its
   * box opened out, and everything below undoes the theme's control-bar
   * treatment of that part for this one instance -- the bar's own buttons are
   * 44px chips with a hover tint, and a hover tint at this size would wash
   * the picture. `border: 0` and a transparent background hold under `none`
   * too, where without them a bare `<button>` would paint the browser's own
   * opaque fill full-bleed over the picture -- the same defect the `none`
   * activation reset above exists to prevent, on a part that reset does not
   * reach.
   *
   * `position` and `z-index` for the reason the control bar carries them —
   * the media declares a layer of its own and an unpositioned box in the same
   * cell paints underneath it. The bar takes the same `z-index` and comes
   * later in the markup, so it wins the overlap and its own buttons keep
   * their clicks.
   *
   * No focus rule: it is not in the tab order (`SurfaceToggle` sets
   * `tabIndex={-1}`), and a pointer press on a `:focus-visible` site leaves no
   * ring. */
  :global(.bench__stage) :global([data-surface-toggle]) {
    grid-area: stack;
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: 100%;
    border-radius: 0;
    background-color: transparent;
    border: 0;
  }

  :global(.bench__stage) :global([data-surface-toggle]:hover) {
    background-color: transparent;
  }

  /* Pinned to the foot of the picture.
   *
   * `position` and `z-index` are what put the bar in front of the picture
   * rather than behind it: the media declares a layer of its own, so a box left
   * unpositioned in the same cell paints underneath it however late it comes in
   * the markup. Stacking here is asked for, not inherited from source order. */
  :global(.bench__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='controls']) {
    grid-area: stack;
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    place-self: end stretch;
  }

  /* The `[hidden]` reset, needed here rather than assumed: the attribute is
   * honoured by a rule in the user-agent stylesheet, and the theme gives this
   * part a `display` of its own, which any author rule beats. So with the theme
   * loaded, the bar the island marks hidden before a player exists would stay
   * on screen unless this says otherwise. Unlayered and unqualified, which is
   * enough — the theme's declaration sits inside a cascade layer and loses to
   * this one whatever the two specificities are. */
  :global(.bench__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='controls'][hidden]) {
    display: none;
  }

  /* The seek input, taken out of the text line it was sitting in.
   *
   * A range input is inline-level, so in `seek-slider` it is laid into a line
   * box and the container grows past it by the descender space under the
   * baseline. The theme then centres the track on the CONTAINER while the input
   * keeps the top of the line box, so the thumb and the bar it runs along do
   * not share a centre. `display: block` removes the line box, so the container
   * is the input's own 44px. The defect is `theme.css`'s and reaches every
   * consumer of the bundled theme, where it is reported rather than patched
   * here; `e2e/thumb-contrast.spec.ts` pins that geometry. */
  :global(.bench__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='seek-slider-input']) {
    display: block;
  }

  /* Plex Mono is set tight relative to its own width, and this is the site's
   * rule for opening it wherever it appears. The theme sets the family from the
   * token above but has no tracking of its own to set. */
  :global(.bench__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='time']) {
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-fn);
  }

  /* The fallback fills its stage rather than sizing itself from the still, so
   * the no-JavaScript frame is the same box the player's is. A link, not a
   * button: it is one, and it says so in its label. */
  :global(.bench__fallback) {
    position: relative;
